          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.085,
            0.599,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": "• Why are lines l and m parallel to each other?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.138,
            0.439,
            0.162
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.8 Alternate Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.17,
            0.847,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": "In Fig. 5.25, $\\angle d$ is called the **alternate angle** of $\\angle f ,$ and $\\angle c$ is the alternate angle of $\\angle e$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.393,
            0.212,
            0.602,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.462,
                0.374,
                0.537,
                0.39
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.25"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.399,
            0.847,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": "You can find the alternate angle of a given angle, say $\\angle f ,$ by first finding the corresponding angle of $\\angle f ,$ which is $\\angle b$ and then finding the vertically opposite angle of $\\angle b ,$ , which is $\\angle d .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.463,
            0.259,
            0.485
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.484,
            0.846,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": "In Fig. 5.25, if $\\angle f$ is $1 2 0 ^ { \\circ }$ what is the measure of its alternate angle $\\angle d ?$ We can find the measure of $\\angle d$ if we know $\\angle b$ because they are vertically opposite angles. Remember, vertically opposite angles are equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.557,
            0.847,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "What is the measure of $\\mathbb { Z } b ?$ It is $1 2 0 ^ { \\circ }$ because it is the corresponding angle of $\\angle f .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.595,
            0.44,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\angle d$ also measures $1 2 0 ^ { \\circ } ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.612,
            0.846,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "In fact, $\\angle f = \\angle b$ irrespective of the measure of $\\angle f .$ Why? Because $\\angle b$ is the corresponding angle of $Z   f .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.65,
            0.847,
            0.704
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, $\\angle b = \\angle d$ irrespective of the measure of $\\angle b$ . Why? Because $\\angle d$ is the vertically opposite angle of ∠b. So, it must always be the $\\angle b .$ case that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.705,
            0.256,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": "\\angle f = \\angle d",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.723,
            0.847,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": "Using our understanding of corresponding angles without any measurements, we have justified that alternate angles are always equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.79,
            0.826,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": "Alternate angles formed by a transversal intersecting a pair of parallel lines are always equal to each other."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.86,
            0.846,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 1:** In Fig. 5.26, parallel lines l and m are intersected by the transversal t. If $\\angle 6$ is $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , what are the measures of the other angles?"
        },
